Behavior Interface
---------------------

The **Behavior** interface defines the contract that every behavior must fulfil. It is parameterized by:

* **D** &mdash; the **BehaviorData** type that carries configuration and state for this behavior.
* **C** &mdash; the **BehavioredComponent** type to which this behavior can be attached.

The interface exposes two accessors:

* `data()` &mdash; returns the **BehaviorData** instance bound to this behavior.
* `component()` &mdash; returns the owning component.


```java
public interface Behavior<D extends BehaviorData, C extends BehavioredComponent<C>> extends Component<Behavior<?, ?>> {

    /**
     * Gets the {@link BehaviorData} used by this Behavior.
     *
     * @return the behavior data
     */
    D data();

    /**
     * Gets the {@link EnhancedComponent} concerned by this Behavior.
     *
     * @return the component
     */
    C component();

}
```

- `Behavior<D, C>` — `D` = configuration data, `C` = host component type (Model, Command, …).
- `data()` — read/write behavior configuration supplied at attach time.
- `component()` — the owning `BehavioredComponent` this behavior augments.


Behavior Data
============================

**BehaviorData** is the data holder associated with a behavior. It carries configuration and runtime state that the behavior needs to operate.

A single BehaviorData can reference one or more **Behavior** classes via the `getBehaviors()` method. This means that adding a BehaviorData to a component can trigger the creation of several related behaviors at once.


```java
@FunctionalInterface
public interface BehaviorData {

    List<Class<? extends Behavior<?, ?>>> getBehaviors();

}
```

- `getBehaviors()` — lists behavior interfaces to create when this data is attached.
- Override `getCustomBehaviors()` on `AbstractBehaviorData` for programmatic additions.


The framework provides **AbstractBehaviorData** (in the `core` module) as a convenient base class. It automatically collects behavior references declared via:

1. The `getCustomBehaviors()` template method (override to add programmatic references).
2. The **@BehaviorDataFor** annotation placed on the data class itself.

**BehaviorDataBase** extends `AbstractBehaviorData` with an empty `getCustomBehaviors()` list, making it the simplest starting point for custom data classes.


### @BehaviorDataFor Annotation ###

This annotation links a BehaviorData class to its Behavior interface at the type level:


```java
@BehaviorDataFor(DockableBehavior.class)
public class Dockable extends BehaviorDataBase { /* ... */ }
```

- `@BehaviorDataFor(DockableBehavior.class)` — one data class maps to one behavior interface.
- At `addBehavior(data)` the framework reads this annotation to instantiate behaviors.


Creating a Custom Behavior
============================

Creating a behavior requires three artifacts: an **interface**, an **implementation**, and a **data class**.

### 1. Define the Behavior Interface ###

Create an interface extending `Behavior<YourData, TargetComponent>`. Mark it with **@RegistrationPoint** so it can participate in the modular registration system:


```java
@RegistrationPoint(exclusive = true)
public interface DockableBehavior extends Behavior<Dockable, Model> {

    default String modelName() {
        return getClass().getSimpleName().replaceAll("Model", "");
    }

    default Image modelIcon() {
        return ComponentImages.DefaultDockableIcon.get();
    }
}
```

- `@RegistrationPoint` — exposes the behavior to the modular registration / facade system.
- `Behavior<Dockable, Model>` — this behavior only attaches to `Model` hosts.
- Default methods — optional API on the behavior interface (icons, labels, etc.).

### 2. Define the BehaviorData ###

Create a data class extending `BehaviorDataBase` and annotate it with **@BehaviorDataFor** to bind it to the behavior interface:


```java
@BehaviorDataFor(DockableBehavior.class)
public class Dockable extends BehaviorDataBase implements Serializable {

    /** The generated serial version uid. */
    private static final long serialVersionUID = 8137109665415403740L;

    private String name;

    private UniqueKey<? extends Model> modelKey;

    public static Dockable create() {
        return new Dockable();
    }

    public String name() {
        return this.name;
    }

    public Dockable name(final String name) {
        this.name = name;
        return this;
    }

    public UniqueKey<? extends Model> modelKey() {
        return this.modelKey;
    }

    public Dockable modelKey(final UniqueKey<? extends Model> modelKey) {
        this.modelKey = modelKey;
        if (!this.modelKey.optionalData().contains(this)) {
            this.modelKey.optionalData().add(this);
        }
        return this;
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        final StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        sb.append(getClass().getSimpleName()).append(" [");
        sb.append(this.name).append(" ").append(this.modelKey.key()).append("]");
        return sb.toString();
    }

}
```

- `@BehaviorDataFor(DockableBehavior.class)` — links data class to behavior implementation.
- `BehaviorDataBase` — collects behavior classes from annotation + `getCustomBehaviors()`.
- Fluent setters (`name()`, `modelKey()`) — configuration passed when attaching to a Model.

### 3. Implement the Behavior ###

Extend **AbstractBehavior** (or **AbstractModelBehavior** for Model-aware behaviors) and annotate with **@Register** to wire the implementation to its interface:


```java
@Register(value = DockableBehavior.class, priority = PriorityLevel.Normal)
public class DockableBehaviorImpl extends AbstractModelBehavior<Dockable> implements DockableBehavior {

    /**
     * {@inheritDoc}
     */
    @Override
    public String modelName() {
        return data().name();
    }

    @Override
    public void initBehavior() {
        // Nothing to init
    }

    @Override
    protected void initInnerComponents() {
    }

}
```

- `@Register(DockableBehavior.class)` — binds implementation to interface for facade lookup.
- `AbstractModelBehavior<Dockable>` — Model-specific base; provides `model()` helpers.
- `initBehavior()` — startup hook once `data()` and `component()` are wired.
- `data().name()` — read configuration supplied when the behavior was attached.

The `initBehavior()` method is called once the behavior is fully initialized (data and component references are resolved). Use it to set up listeners, bindings, or any startup logic.


Attaching Behaviors to Components
============================

Behaviors are attached to any component implementing the **BehavioredComponent** interface (all standard Models, Services, and Commands qualify).

### Programmatic Attachment ###

You can attach a behavior at any point during a component's lifecycle:

* **By Behavior class** &mdash; `addBehavior(MyBehavior.class)` creates the behavior with default data.
* **By BehaviorData** &mdash; `addBehavior(myDataInstance)` creates all behaviors referenced by the data.

```java
@Override
protected void initModel() {
    addBehavior(Dockable.create().name("Tools").modelKey(getModelKey()));
    final DockableBehavior dock = getBehavior(DockableBehavior.class);
}
```

- `addBehavior(data)` — creates every behavior listed on the `BehaviorData` instance.
- `getBehavior(DockableBehavior.class)` — typed access after attach; same lifecycle as host Model.

Once attached, you can query and access behaviors:

* `hasBehavior(MyBehavior.class)` &mdash; checks if the behavior is present.
* `getBehavior(MyBehavior.class)` &mdash; retrieves the behavior instance.
* `getBehaviorData(MyBehavior.class)` &mdash; retrieves the data bound to a behavior.
* `getBehaviorsAware(MyAwareInterface.class)` &mdash; retrieves all behaviors implementing a given aware interface.


```java
public interface BehavioredComponent<C extends Component<C>> extends Component<C> {

    /**
     * Return true if the component contains the {@link Behavior} class provided.
     *
     * @param behaviorClass the class of the {@link Behavior} to check
     *
     * @return true if the component has got this behavior
     */
    boolean hasBehavior(final Class<? extends Behavior<?, ?>> behaviorClass);

    /**
     * Add a behavior to the component by providing the {@link Behavior} class.
     *
     * @param behaviorClass the class of the behavior to add
     *
     * @return the current component to chain method call
     */
    <BD extends BehaviorData, B extends Behavior<BD, ?>> C addBehavior(final Class<B> behaviorClass);

    /**
     * Add a behavior to the component by providing a {@link BehaviorData}.
     *
     * The BehaviorData allow to add several {@link Behavior} if many of them rare reference by the BehaviorData.
     *
     * @param data the Behavior Data that references Behavior classes to add
     *
     * @return the current component to chain method call
     */
    <BD extends BehaviorData> C addBehavior(final BD data);

    /**
     * Return the {@link Behavior} instance created for the given Behavior type.
     *
     * @param behaviorClass the Behavior type to get
     *
     * @return the Behavior instance attached to the component
     */
    <BD extends BehaviorData, B extends Behavior<BD, ?>> B getBehavior(final Class<B> behaviorClass);

    /**
     * Return the list of {@link BehaviorAware} filtered using given parameter.
     * 
     * @param awareClass the class that shall be implemented by behaviors returned
     * 
     * @return the list of {@link BehaviorAware}
     */
    <BA extends BehaviorAware> List<BA> getBehaviorsAware(final Class<BA> awareClass);

    /**
     * Return the {@link BehaviorData} used by the given Behavior type.
     *
     * @param behaviorClass the Behavior type that use the searched data
     *
     * @return the Behavior Data used by the given Behavior
     */
    <BD extends BehaviorData, B extends Behavior<BD, ?>> BD getBehaviorData(final Class<B> behaviorClass);

}
```


Built-in Behaviors
============================

JRebirth ships several ready-to-use behaviors in the `component` and `security` modules.

### DockableBehavior ###

The **DockableBehavior** (in the `component` module) makes a Model dockable within a tabbed or panel-based layout. Each dockable component exposes a `modelName()` and `modelIcon()` used by the docking container.

Its data class, **Dockable**, carries the panel name and a `UniqueKey` pointing to the Model to display.


```java
@RegistrationPoint(exclusive = true)
public interface DockableBehavior extends Behavior<Dockable, Model> {

    default String modelName() {
        return getClass().getSimpleName().replaceAll("Model", "");
    }

    default Image modelIcon() {
        return ComponentImages.DefaultDockableIcon.get();
    }
}
```


```java
@BehaviorDataFor(DockableBehavior.class)
public class Dockable extends BehaviorDataBase implements Serializable {

    /** The generated serial version uid. */
